<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret Atwood</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">born 1939</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret Atwood is a Canadian novelist, poet and critic. Her novels include The Edible Woman, Cat's Eye, The Handmaid's Tale, a dystopia published in 1985, and The Blind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassin</a>, which won the Booker Prize in 2000; she won it again in 2019 for The Testaments. Her critical study Survival helped define the study of Canadian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-atwood.jpg" alt="Margaret Atwood" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collision Conf · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_Atwood_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
